 some of these groups  why do i think that there is vaccine  reluctance or skepticism well first of  all  i just don't buy this idea that's being  put around and ministers say this  that oh it's all about language and the  communication's not reaching them  if you look at the ons data the  proportion of people in this country who  do not speak english well enough  to understand that kind of information  is about what is 1.3  the highest level of language lack of  language proficiency  is uh bangladeshi women five percent  so this idea that basically it's all  about giving it the information to them  in their languages and so on brackets  they're all a bit backward  i just hate i really despise it because  actually it's a good way for official  them  to pretend that actually it's the fault  of these  minority communities and secondly what  it suggests  is that these people just don't know  what's going on if they  you know if we educated them a bit  better they they'd think the same as us  and it's a failure to recognize that  actually some of the  communities involved just take a  different  view about risk and about levels  of uh the value of vaccine and there's  one other thing which may explain  partly why the black figure let's call  it  though that covers a whole multitude of  kinds of people is so high  and that is simply distrust of authority  uh something similar is true in the  united states where african americans  are displaying significant levels of  resistance  to taking the vaccine and what we think  is going on there is very simply  we don't believe you and actually uh  everything you've done in the past has  really shafted us  and there's one other small point which  perhaps um  we ought to think about um and which you  know i'm i'm  completely pro the vaccine well one of  the questions that one might  ask actually if you were from some  minority backgrounds is  has this been tested on people like me  and actually what we know about the way  that the  pharma company carry out their testing  is that they always do  have uh a deficit in  the proportion of people from minorities  you're a another distinguished british  uh  citizen from a caribbean background jeff  palmer so jeff hammer who uh was writing  in the mail  this week he also mentioned the issue of  trust  um he also wrote and i've  remained to be convinced about this you  take that there were  there were just too many white people up  on that stage  from public school backgrounds and that  was the reason  can can that really explain 72 percent  well uh full disclosure jeff um  has been a family friend interestingly  enough  from the time he was a great friend of  mine  older brother so i've known him since i  was six years old  and i have great regard for him i think  he's a  you know he's a brilliant scientist and  so forth i think he's got his  got it around his neck on this one i  think actually the idea that if you just  had a few black faces on the platform  uh it's a variety of the same thing  that actually um if you  basically talk to the chiefs then the  chiefs will talk to the indians  and then it'll all be all right  i just think this is nonsense the truth  of the matter is that people are fully  conscious by and large  of what they're being told but  in for substantial reasons some to do  with cultural background maybe religious  beliefs  but also skepticism about what they're  being told by  any authority in this country they are  not  convinced why does this matter well it  matters because if you're going to  remedy  that simply to giving them the same  information  just in another language isn't going to  crack it you've got to address  the fact that people have different  points of view and this in a way goes to  the heart of a debate that we've been  having 20 years about multiculturalism  that you know you can all go kumbaya and  say okay we're all  the same under the skin and all the rest  of it but there will come a moment  and we may be coming to one where what  we discover  is that there are some people who  sincerely believe something  which is completely at odds with what  the  majority of the country believes and  what the majority of the country  believes  to be at odds with the interest of the  community as a whole  and at that point somebody's got to make  a decision  about how you deal with that  well to ask my favorite leninist  question  what is to be done  well i think uh the first thing is that  we you know you know that i bang on  about this  we've got to have proper information i  mean right now  everybody says constantly uh their  minorities are at greater risk well  actually the latest data  from the nhs on mortality shows  that the proportion of people who have  died who are white  is 86 percent which is exactly  the number that white people represent  in the population as a whole  so first of all we need to stop uh  spinning a narrative which is basically  the one that people would like to  to be true because it solves all sorts  of problems and deal with the fact and  that needs some work  secondly um and and also if you're going  to persuade people you've got to show  them some actual data  so i would say that we first need to  understand  who is at risk and secondly we need to  understand better  which communities are truly reluctant  saying black people are more reluctant  which includes somalis and ghanaians  and bardems who all have completely  different outlooks  you know some might be muslims some  might be christians we need to  understand  who is really vaccine skeptic and  why before we uh tackle the problem  so i think we need to do a bit more  research and be a bit more honest about  this before we start throwing solutions  which frankly will have no impact into  the pot i mentioned that i was a  skeptical of jeff palmer's complaint  about too many white too many public  school boys giving out the message  um but i do also see there is some  validity  in giving up